git margin. The lone poll showing a four-point race and DiLorenzo's fundraising advantage justify tempering the outlook rather than treating it as safe.", "watch": "Fresh polling or a late shift in the national climate toward Republicans, amplified by DiLorenzo's cash advantage and heavy outside spending, could move this toward the tossup column.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's 1st District is an open seat after Chris Pappas left the House race to run for Senate, with Stefany Shaheen carrying the Democratic banner against Republican Anthony DiLorenzo. The district leans Democratic by about two points, and the national climate favors Democrats this year. Most race raters call the contest Likely Democratic, with a couple rating it Lean Democratic and others placing it in the Safe or Solid column. Prediction markets put Democratic odds at roughly 95 percent or better.\n\nThe evidence is not uniformly one-sided. The only public poll, from the University of New Hampshire in late September, showed Shaheen ahead by four points among likely voters. DiLorenzo has out-raised Shaheen in candidate fundraising, though outside groups have spent heavily on her behalf. National figures from both parties, including Speaker Johnson and Leader Jeffries, have campaigned in the district, underscoring its role in the fight for House control.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8928, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8695, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.844, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.871, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8487, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8802, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8452, "margin": 6.0